insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 1, 'Rock physics is described in module 1 as a bridge. Which two measurements does it stand between?', '["A well log, which reports rock properties over a small volume along one borehole, and a seismic amplitude, which reports an impedance contrast over a large volume.","A core plug measurement and a well log, both made in the same borehole.","A seismic amplitude and a production rate measured at surface.","A pressure gauge reading and a temperature reading taken at the same depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rock physics connects the small volume property measurement made in the well to the large volume impedance contrast the seismic wave records. The two are not measurements of the same quantity, so no arithmetic converts one into the other without a model.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 2, 'Module 1 lists five mechanisms that can brighten a seismic reflection. Which of the following is not one of them?', '["Gas filling some of the pore space.","A deeper oil water contact in the same structure.","A cleaner sand with less clay in the frame.","A thin bed tuning constructively at the seismic wavelength."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The five named mechanisms are gas in the pores, higher porosity, a cleaner frame, a harder overlying shale, and thin bed tuning. The depth of a contact is not among them, and the point of the list is that a single amplitude cannot separate the causes that are on it.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 3, 'In what order does the Associate tier build its calculation?', '["Build the mineral frame, state the conditions, mix the pore fluids, then build the three fluids.","Mix the pore fluids, build the three fluids, state the conditions, then build the mineral frame.","State the conditions, build the three fluids, build the mineral frame, then mix the pore fluids.","Build the three fluids, mix the pore fluids, state the conditions, then build the mineral frame."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each step consumes the output of the one before it, so the sequence cannot be reordered. The conditions must be fixed before any fluid can be evaluated, and both brine and gas must exist before they can be mixed.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 4, 'Which line states the Ekene brine and gas bulk moduli at 60 degC and 25 MPa with the units this course uses?', '["Brine 2.6978112899395996 MPa, gas 55.71865290286663 GPa.","Brine 55.71865290286663 GPa, gas 2.6978112899395996 MPa.","Brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, gas 55.71865290286663 GPa.","Brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, gas 55.71865290286663 MPa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Brine and oil moduli are quoted in GPa and the gas modulus is quoted in MPa, because the gas value would otherwise have its leading figures pushed past the decimal point. Mixing the two units in one comparison is the classic beginner error in this course, and the last option makes the gas look stiffer than the brine.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 5, 'The course states that brine is 48.42 times stiffer than the gas at the Ekene conditions. What does that ratio explain?', '["Why replacing brine with gas changes a rock enough for the change to be visible on ordinary seismic data.","Why gas rises above oil and oil rises above water in a reservoir.","Why the mineral frame is much stiffer than any of the pore fluids.","Why brine density is above 1000 kg/m3 while gas density is below it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fluid detection works because the pore contents become nearly fifty times more compressible when gas replaces brine, which slows the rock and changes the reflection at its top. Buoyancy is a density effect and is a separate matter from the modulus ratio.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 6, 'The Ekene specification has seven lines. Which pair describes the state the fluids are in rather than what they are made of?', '["Brine salinity 0.035 and gas gravity 0.6.","Temperature 60 degC and pressure 25 MPa.","Solution gas to oil ratio 50 L/L and oil density 0.85 g/cc.","The 70 percent quartz and 30 percent clay frame."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Temperature and pressure define the state a fluid is in, while salinity, gas gravity, surface oil density and GOR describe composition, and the quartz to clay split describes the solid. A fluid property depends on both groups, which is why all seven lines have to travel with any number quoted from them.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 7, 'At 60 degC and gravity 0.6, the Ekene gas has a bulk modulus of 17.7074 MPa at 10 MPa, 55.7187 MPa at 25 MPa and 111.3929 MPa at 40 MPa. What does that show?', '["Gas properties are constant enough to be read from a handbook table.","Gas softens as it is compressed, because the molecules are forced closer together.","Gas stiffens steeply with pressure, so a gas bulk modulus cannot be quoted without the pressure it was evaluated at.","Only the density of gas responds to pressure, while the bulk modulus stays fixed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both the density and the bulk modulus of gas climb steeply with pressure, because squeezing a compressible fluid packs more mass into the same volume and makes it harder to squeeze further. Treating gas properties as table constants is the error this table is meant to end.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 8, 'Why is the Ekene SAND specified at 60 degC and 25 MPa rather than at a depth in metres?', '["Depth is harder to measure accurately than temperature or pressure.","Depth is only relevant once the mineral frame has been mixed.","Depth cannot be converted into a pressure under any circumstances.","The physics responds to pressure and temperature, and two fields at the same depth in different basins can have different gradients and therefore different fluid properties."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Depth is a proxy for the conditions that actually drive the fluid properties, and the mapping from depth to conditions depends on local gradients. Stating the conditions makes the calculation reproducible by anyone who has them.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 9, 'In this tier, what does the 25 MPa in the Ekene specification refer to?', '["The pore pressure, which is the pressure the fluid in the pore space is under.","The effective stress carried by the mineral frame.","The overburden stress from the whole rock column above the reservoir.","The pressure drop measured across the reservoir during a well test."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the Associate tier the 25 MPa acts on the fluids, so it is the pore pressure. Effective stress on the frame is a different quantity that drives a different set of effects and is introduced at the Professional tier.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 10, 'A bulk modulus is the ratio of an applied pressure to the fractional volume change it produces. What units does it therefore carry?', '["Units of density, such as kg/m3, because a volume appears in the definition.","Units of pressure, such as GPa or MPa, because the denominator is a fraction and carries no units.","Units of velocity, such as m/s, because it controls how fast a wave travels.","No units at all, because it is the ratio of two quantities."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fractional volume change is dimensionless, so the modulus inherits the units of the pressure applied. A modulus quoted in kg/m3 is a sign that a density and a modulus have been swapped somewhere.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 11, 'What is the shear modulus of the Ekene live oil at reservoir conditions?', '["1.1427945726905131 GPa, the same as its bulk modulus.","A small positive value that grows with the dissolved gas content.","Zero, because no fluid supports a static shear stress.","777.0630099023522 kg/m3, taken straight from the engine output."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every fluid in this course has a shear modulus of exactly zero, since a fluid flows rather than leaning and holding when it is sheared. Expecting a fluid to carry some shear stiffness is a common beginner error, and the last option also confuses a density with a modulus.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 12, 'Which moduli appear in the expression for the shear velocity of an elastic solid?', '["The bulk modulus only, together with the density.","The bulk modulus and the shear modulus, together with the density.","Neither modulus, since shear velocity depends on density alone.","The shear modulus only, together with the density."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A shear wave changes shape without changing volume, so the bulk modulus never enters its velocity. Compressional velocity involves both moduli, because a compressional wave both squeezes and distorts the material.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 13, 'For a fluid the compressional velocity reduces to the square root of K over rho. Which statement about the Ekene fluids is correct?', '["The engine returns 1628.0555893189182 m/s for the brine and 1212.7072294996883 m/s for the live oil, and it returns no compressional velocity for the gas.","The engine returns a compressional velocity for all three fluids, with the gas the fastest of them.","The engine returns a compressional velocity only for the gas, because gas is the most compressible fluid.","The engine returns 1628.0555893189182 m/s for the live oil and 1212.7072294996883 m/s for the brine."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The brine and live oil velocities come back from the engine, while no gas velocity is returned, so the course quotes none. The last option swaps the two velocities that are returned.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 14, 'Quartz has K 36.6 GPa and mu 45.0 GPa, while calcite has K 76.8 GPa and mu 32.0 GPa. What does that pair of rows show?', '["Calcite is stiffer than quartz in every respect, so a limestone is always the faster rock.","A material cannot be ranked as generally hard or generally soft, because the two moduli rank materials differently.","The shear modulus of a mineral can always be estimated from its bulk modulus.","Quartz and calcite are close enough that the distinction does not affect a mixed frame."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Calcite is much stiffer than quartz under compression and much softer under shear, so the ranking flips between the two moduli. That is why bulk and shear modulus are carried as separate properties rather than one being inferred from the other.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-rock-physics-and-the-seismic-bridge', 15, 'The Ekene reservoir turns out to be at 40 MPa rather than 25 MPa. Which of the six capstone numbers is unaffected?', '["The brine density of 1017.8249875 kg/m3.","The gas bulk modulus of 55.71865290286663 MPa.","The frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa.","The live oil density of 777.0630099023522 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The frame comes from the mineral constants and the 70 to 30 quartz to clay split, neither of which depends on pressure at this tier. All five fluid derived numbers move when the pressure moves.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 1, 'Which inputs does the Batzle and Wang brine relation take?', '["Temperature and pressure only.","Temperature, pressure and gas gravity.","Salinity and depth.","Temperature, pressure and salinity."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Brine takes temperature, pressure and salinity, and returns density, bulk modulus and compressional velocity. Gas gravity belongs to the gas relation, and depth is never an input, because it is only a proxy for the conditions.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 2, 'Which inputs does the Batzle and Wang live oil relation take?', '["Temperature, pressure, the surface oil density and the solution gas to oil ratio.","Temperature, pressure and salinity.","The surface oil density alone, since API gravity fixes everything else.","Temperature and the solution gas to oil ratio only."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Live oil needs both state variables and both composition variables, and leaving out the GOR turns a live oil calculation into a dead oil one. Salinity belongs to the brine relation.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 3, 'Which of the three fluids responds most strongly to a change in pressure, and why?', '["Brine, because the dissolved salt is squeezed out of solution under pressure.","Gas, because it is highly compressible, so its modulus runs from 17.7074 MPa at 10 MPa to 111.3929 MPa at 40 MPa.","Live oil, because pressure forces more gas out of solution as it rises.","All three respond equally, since pressure acts on every fluid in the pore space."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Squeezing a compressible fluid both packs more mass into the same volume and makes it harder to squeeze further, so gas density and gas modulus both climb steeply. There is no pressure regime in which a gas property can be quoted without its pressure.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 4, 'At 25 MPa and 0.035 salinity the brine bulk modulus is 2.511437 GPa at 20 degC, 2.697811 GPa at 60 degC, 2.689136 GPa at 80 degC and 2.629247 GPa at 100 degC. What does that pattern show?', '["Brine bulk modulus falls steadily as the brine is heated.","Brine bulk modulus rises steadily as the brine is heated.","Brine bulk modulus is not monotonic in temperature, since it peaks between 60 and 80 degC and falls away on both sides.","Brine bulk modulus is effectively constant over this range of temperature."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The modulus rises to a peak between 60 and 80 degC and then declines, so there is no single direction to memorise and the relation has to be evaluated at the conditions in hand. Assuming that heating always softens a brine is the error this table corrects.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 5, 'At 60 degC and 25 MPa the Ekene brine gives 994.0097 kg/m3 and 2.534420 GPa at 0 weight percent salt, and 1063.6947 kg/m3 and 3.034253 GPa at 10 weight percent. What does dissolved salt do?', '["It raises the density and lowers the bulk modulus.","It lowers the density and raises the bulk modulus.","It changes the density while leaving the bulk modulus untouched.","It raises both the density and the bulk modulus together."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dissolved ions organise the water more tightly than pure water organises itself, so the brine becomes both heavier and harder to compress. That is why brine density and brine modulus move in the same direction when salinity changes.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 6, 'Which fluid in this course is the stiffest, and how firm is that ordering?', '["Brine, at 2.6978112899395996 GPa, and the ordering of brine above oil above gas never reverses under the conditions this course visits.","Gas, at 55.71865290286663 MPa, because gas is under the highest pressure in the reservoir.","Live oil, at 1.1427945726905131 GPa, because it holds both liquid and dissolved gas.","The three are close enough that the ordering depends on the temperature chosen."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Brine is the stiff fluid, live oil sits in the middle at 1.1427945726905131 GPa, and gas is soft at 55.71865290286663 MPa. Reading the gas value as though its MPa were GPa is what makes the second option look plausible.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 7, 'In the brine temperature table the bulk modulus peaks between 60 and 80 degC, while the compressional velocity reaches its highest listed value of 1633.6850 m/s at 80 degC. Why does the velocity peak later than the modulus?', '["The engine evaluates velocity on a coarser temperature grid than the modulus.","Velocity is the square root of K over rho, and the density keeps falling with temperature, which pushes velocity up for a while after the modulus has started to drop.","Velocity depends on the shear modulus, which peaks at a higher temperature than the bulk modulus.","The velocity peak is an artefact and the two peaks should coincide."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both the modulus in the numerator and the falling density in the denominator act on velocity, so the two effects place the velocity maximum deeper into the temperature range. The shear modulus plays no part, because a fluid has none.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 8, 'Why is the Ekene gas bulk modulus quoted as 55.71865290286663 MPa rather than in GPa?', '["Gas moduli are always reported in MPa by international convention, whatever their size.","MPa is the unit the reservoir pressure is given in, so the modulus has to match it.","It sits far below the brine and oil moduli, so it reads clearly in MPa while its leading figures would fall past the decimal point in GPa.","The engine cannot return a value in GPa for a compressible fluid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unit change is a warning label telling you this fluid is on a different scale from the other two. Carrying a gas modulus in MPa into a comparison against a brine modulus in GPa without noticing is the classic error this course guards against.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 9, 'The Ekene gas has a density of 172.66679461728904 kg/m3 at 60 degC and 25 MPa. What does that tell you about it?', '["It is nearly weightless, so its contribution to the rock density can be ignored.","It must have condensed into a separate liquid phase at this pressure.","It has been mis-evaluated, because a gas density cannot exceed 100 kg/m3.","It is a dense supercritical fluid, a substantial fraction of the brine density of 1017.8249875 kg/m3 rather than a negligible mass."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Above its critical temperature and pressure the gas has no distinct liquid phase and behaves as a single fluid with a liquid like density and a gas like compressibility. Carrying the picture of thin surface gas into a deep reservoir understates the rock density and therefore the reflection.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 10, 'Two prospects in one basin hold the same gas at the same saturation, one at 10 MPa and one at 40 MPa. Which produces the stronger seismic fluid effect?', '["The shallow one, because its gas modulus of 17.7074 MPa contrasts more strongly with the brine it replaces than the deep value of 111.3929 MPa does.","The deep one, because the higher pressure makes the gas more compressible.","They are the same, because the saturation and the gas composition are the same.","The deep one, because gas density rises with pressure and density alone drives the reflection."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gas stiffens steeply with pressure, so the gap between the gas and the brine it replaces narrows with depth and the fluid effect weakens. Bright spot workflows that work shallow in a basin often stop working deeper for this reason.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 11, 'At 60 degC and 25 MPa the Ekene gas at gravity 0.6 gives 172.6668 kg/m3 and 55.7187 MPa, while gravity 1.0 gives 337.4248 kg/m3 and 119.8581 MPa. What follows for interpretation?', '["A wetter gas is lighter and softer, so it produces a stronger seismic signature.","A wetter gas is both denser and stiffer, so at the same depth and saturation it produces a weaker seismic signature than a dry gas.","Gas gravity affects the density only, so the seismic signature is unchanged.","Gas gravity matters only above 40 MPa, so it can be ignored at 25 MPa."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Gas gravity stands in for composition, and heavier gas carries both more mass and more resistance to compression. An analogue field that produced a spectacular bright spot on dry gas is not a safe guide for a prospect expected to be wetter.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 12, 'What distinguishes live oil from dead oil?', '["Live oil is oil that is still flowing, while dead oil is what is left behind after production.","Live oil contains free gas bubbles, while dead oil is a single phase.","Live oil is oil at reservoir conditions with gas still dissolved in it, while dead oil has had that gas come out of solution.","Live oil is measured at surface, while dead oil is measured in the pore space."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The dissolved gas is in solution and the mixture behaves as a single phase, so the distinction is about composition rather than about bubbles or flow. The oil in the pore space is the live one, and it is what a seismic wave passes through.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 13, 'At 60 degC and 25 MPa the Ekene oil gives 820.9856 kg/m3 and 1.475341 GPa at GOR 0, 777.0630 kg/m3 and 1.142795 GPa at GOR 50, and 693.8631 kg/m3 and 0.715855 GPa at GOR 150. What does dissolved gas do?', '["It makes the oil lighter and stiffer.","It makes the oil heavier and softer.","It changes the density while leaving the bulk modulus essentially unchanged.","It makes the oil lighter and much softer at the same time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dissolved gas molecules push the liquid structure apart, which lowers the density, and they introduce compressible material into a nearly incompressible liquid, which lowers the bulk modulus. The two effects are the same event seen from two sides.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 14, 'An interpreter uses a dead oil bulk modulus of 1.475341 GPa for a reservoir whose live oil value is 1.142795 GPa. What is the consequence?', '["The pore fluid is treated as stiffer than it is, so the modelled rock comes out too fast and the modelled fluid effect is understated.","The pore fluid is treated as softer than it is, so the modelled fluid effect is overstated.","The error cancels out, because the density error runs the opposite way.","There is no consequence, because the bulk modulus of the fluid does not reach the modelled reflection."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using 1.475341 GPa where 1.142795 GPa is correct puts too much stiffness in the pore space, which raises the modelled velocity and shrinks the modelled contrast against a brine case. Treating dead oil and live oil as interchangeable is one of the three habitual errors named in the lesson.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-reservoir-fluids', 15, 'Set the three Ekene fluid moduli side by side: brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, live oil 1.1427945726905131 GPa and gas 55.71865290286663 MPa. What is the conclusion the rest of the course rests on?', '["The three are evenly spaced, so any fluid change produces a similar seismic response.","Gas is the outlier by more than an order of magnitude, and that gap is what makes saturation detectable at all.","Live oil is the outlier, because it is the only fluid with dissolved gas in it.","Brine is the outlier, because it is the only fluid whose modulus exceeds 2 GPa."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Brine and oil sit on the same scale and in the same unit, while gas is far below both and is quoted in MPa. If all three moduli were within a factor of two of each other, fluid substitution would barely change a rock and seismic hydrocarbon detection would not work.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 1, 'In this course, the mineral frame of the Ekene sand refers to which of the following?', '["The pore space of the rock, described by its porosity and by which fluids fill it","The dry rock including its pore geometry, whose modulus is softer than that of the solid material","The solid mineral material of the rock, summarised by a mixed bulk modulus, shear modulus and density","The seismic response of the rock once the pore fluid has been mixed by Wood''s equation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The frame at this tier is what is left when the fluid is taken out, described by three numbers for the solid mixture. The dry rock with its pore space is a different and softer quantity that belongs to a higher tier.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 2, 'Using the engine mineral table, what are the shear moduli of quartz and clay?', '["45.0 GPa for quartz and 6.9 GPa for clay","36.6 GPa for quartz and 20.9 GPa for clay","6.9 GPa for quartz and 45.0 GPa for clay","32.0 GPa for quartz and 45.0 GPa for clay"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table gives quartz a shear modulus of 45.0 GPa and clay 6.9 GPa. Quoting 36.6 and 20.9 GPa is the classic slip of reading the bulk modulus column instead of the shear column.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 3, 'The Ekene frame is 70 percent quartz at 2650 kg/m3 and 30 percent clay at 2580 kg/m3. What frame density does the engine return?', '["2650 kg/m3","2629 kg/m3","2580 kg/m3","2710 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Density mixes exactly linearly, so 0.7 x 2650 + 0.3 x 2580 = 2629 kg/m3, which is what the engine returns. The values 2650 and 2580 kg/m3 are the pure mineral end members and 2710 kg/m3 is calcite.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 4, 'Why can the frame density be stated exactly while the frame bulk modulus can only be bracketed between two bounds?', '["Because density is measured in the laboratory while moduli are only ever modelled","Because the engine stores density in kg/m3 and moduli in Pa","Because mineral densities are known to more decimal places than mineral moduli","Because mass and volume add regardless of how the minerals are arranged, while stiffness depends on that arrangement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Density is blind to geometry, so the volume weighted sum is exact. Stiffness depends on whether the soft mineral sits in the load path, which is why the moduli need bounds rather than a formula.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 5, 'What does the Voigt bound assume about the constituents of a mixture?', '["That every constituent suffers the same strain, which is the stiff arrangement","That every constituent suffers the same stress, which is the stiff arrangement","That the constituents sit in series along the load path","That the soft mineral carries none of the load at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Voigt is the equal strain assumption, equivalent to elements in parallel, and it gives the arithmetic average and the upper bound. Equal stress is the Reuss assumption, and swapping the two is the commonest error in this topic.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 6, 'For the 70/30 quartz and clay frame, what is the Voigt bulk modulus?', '["29.868801 GPa","30.87940062475596 GPa","31.890000 GPa","33.570000 GPa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The arithmetic average is 0.7 x 36.6 + 0.3 x 20.9 = 31.890000 GPa. The value 29.868801 GPa is the Reuss bulk bound, 30.87940062475596 GPa is the VRH average, and 33.570000 GPa is the Voigt bound for the shear modulus.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 7, 'The Reuss shear modulus of the Ekene frame is far below its Voigt shear modulus. What is the Reuss value and what drives it down?', '["25.25472176759411 GPa, driven down by averaging the two bounds together","16.939444 GPa, driven down because a harmonic average is dominated by its smallest input","33.570000 GPa, driven down by the 30 percent clay fraction","6.9 GPa, driven down to the clay value because clay controls the load path"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic average of 45.0 and 6.9 GPa at a 70/30 split gives 16.939444 GPa, because compliances add and the soft phase brings the largest term into the sum. It does not fall all the way to the clay value of 6.9 GPa.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 8, 'How is the Voigt-Reuss-Hill average defined?', '["The geometric mean of the Voigt and Reuss bounds","The Reuss bound corrected by the volume fraction of the stiffer mineral","The bound that a measured modulus is required to lie closest to","The arithmetic mean of the Voigt and Reuss bounds"]'::jsonb, 3, 'VRH is half the sum of the two bounds and nothing else. There is no fitted constant and no correction term anywhere in it.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 9, 'Averaging the two bulk modulus bounds of 31.890000 and 29.868801 GPa by hand gives 30.8794005 GPa. What does the engine report for the VRH bulk modulus of this frame?', '["30.87940062475596 GPa","30.8794005 GPa exactly, because the engine performs the same arithmetic on the same printed bounds","61.758801 GPa","29.868801 GPa"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine carries the Reuss bound at full machine precision rather than at six decimal places, so it returns 30.87940062475596 GPa. The hand result agrees to eight significant figures and sits far inside the graded tolerance of 0.05 GPa, which is why the hand check is still worth running. Answering 61.758801 GPa is the error of forgetting to halve the sum.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 10, 'Which statement about the VRH average is correct?', '["It is a physical law derived from the elasticity of two phase mixtures","It describes the particular arrangement in which clay coats every grain contact","It is a pragmatic estimate, adopted because the midpoint of the bounds usually approximates measured aggregates well","It is the tightest bound that can be placed on the modulus of an isotropic mixture"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hill observed that the midpoint approximates real aggregates, which is empirical support rather than a derivation, and no arrangement of minerals corresponds to the VRH formula. Treating VRH as a law is what leads people to quote it without its bounds.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 11, 'How do the two bound spreads for the Ekene frame compare?', '["Both spreads are close to a factor of two, so neither VRH value is well constrained","The bulk moduli sit about 6.8 percent apart while the shear moduli sit nearly a factor of two apart","The shear moduli sit about 6.8 percent apart while the bulk moduli sit nearly a factor of two apart","Both spreads are under ten percent, so both VRH values are well constrained"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bulk bounds of 31.890000 and 29.868801 GPa sit about 6.8 percent apart, an exact ratio of 1.067669, while the shear bounds of 33.570000 and 16.939444 GPa sit nearly a factor of two apart, an exact ratio of 1.981765.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 12, 'What causes the shear bounds of this frame to sit so much further apart than the bulk bounds?', '["The engine applies the 70/30 split to shear before it applies it to bulk","The harmonic average becomes unstable whenever the volume fractions are unequal","Clay has no shear strength at all, so the Reuss shear bound collapses","Quartz is about 6.5 times stiffer in shear than clay, while their bulk moduli are within a factor of two of each other"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bound width is set by how far apart the end members sit. Quartz and clay differ as 45.0 against 6.9 GPa in shear but only as 36.6 against 20.9 GPa in bulk, so the choice of averaging rule matters far more for shear.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 13, 'You are handed the frame bulk modulus and the frame shear modulus for this sand, both printed to fourteen digits. How should you treat them?', '["Use the bulk modulus with confidence and carry the shear modulus with its bounds, testing whether any decision survives the full range","Treat both with equal confidence, since both came out of the same averaging rule","Discard the shear modulus, because a wide bound spread makes it unusable","Prefer the shear modulus, because its wider bounds make it the more conservative choice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The same rule produced a narrow spread for bulk and a spread of nearly a factor of two for shear, so treating them as equally solid is the error to avoid. The shear value remains usable as long as its range is propagated rather than hidden behind the printed digits.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 14, 'A core measurement returns a shear modulus of 45.0 GPa for a rock you have modelled as 70 percent quartz and 30 percent clay. What does that tell you?', '["Nothing, because measured moduli routinely fall outside the Voigt and Reuss bounds","That the rock is stiffer than VRH predicted, which confirms an arrangement close to the Voigt limit","That something is wrong, because no arrangement of these minerals in these proportions can exceed the Voigt bound of 33.570000 GPa","That the Reuss bound of 16.939444 GPa should be used for this rock instead"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A real rock has to lie between the bounds, and 45.0 GPa is the pure quartz value, which a rock containing 30 percent clay cannot reach. The measurement, the assumed mineralogy or the sample identification is wrong, and this bracket test costs nothing to run.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-mineral-frame', 15, 'In which rock would the shear modulus tend toward the Reuss value of 16.939444 GPa rather than the Voigt value of 33.570000 GPa?', '["One in which clay sits as isolated pods inside a welded quartz framework","One in which clay films coat every grain contact and sit in the load path","One containing more than 30 percent clay by volume, whatever its arrangement","One in which the quartz grains are cemented to each other by further quartz"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Clay in the load path puts the soft phase in series, which is the compliant Reuss arrangement, while isolated clay pods let quartz carry the load through and tend toward Voigt. Both rocks have the same 70/30 mineralogy and the same frame density of 2629 kg/m3.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 1, 'Wood''s equation combines the pore fluid phases in which way?', '["Bulk modulus linearly and density harmonically","Bulk modulus harmonically, as a Reuss average over the phases, and density linearly","Both bulk modulus and density harmonically","Both bulk modulus and density linearly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The moduli combine through their reciprocals while the densities combine as a saturation weighted sum. Reversing the two halves gives a modulus far too high and a density that no longer sits where the saturations put it.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 2, 'Why is the Reuss average the physical answer for a well mixed pore fluid rather than merely a lower bound?', '["Because fluid moduli are always much smaller than mineral moduli","Because the two phases are present in unequal saturations","Because Wood derived it for gas alone and it was later extended to brine","Because fluids carry no shear stress, so the phases equalise pressure and the equal stress assumption is what actually happens"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Equal stress is one extreme among many possible geometries in a mineral mixture, but it is the real state of a well mixed pore fluid. The same harmonic formula is therefore a bound in one setting and a result in the other.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 3, 'What condition does Wood''s equation require, and what happens when that condition fails?', '["The phases must be mixed far below the seismic wavelength with pressure equalising within a wave cycle, and patchy saturation makes the rock stiffer than Wood predicts","The phases must be separated into patches, and uniform mixing makes the rock stiffer than Wood predicts","The saturations must be equal, and unequal saturations make the formula invalid","It holds at any saturation and any distribution, which is why it is used everywhere"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Uniform saturation is what makes pressure equalisation, and therefore the Reuss average, correct. In patchy saturation the pressure cannot equalise across a patch, so the response climbs toward the stiff side and Wood understates the modulus.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 4, 'Before the two compliance terms at Sw 0.8 can be added together, what has to happen to the brine and gas moduli?', '["Nothing, because a saturation divided by a modulus is dimensionless","The gas modulus is converted to GPa and the brine modulus is left as reported","Both are brought to the same unit, for example brine at 2697811289.9395995 Pa and gas at 55718652.90286663 Pa","Both are converted to MPa and the resulting sum is then read as GPa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brine is reported in GPa and gas in MPa, and a GPa term cannot be added to an MPa term. Adding them as printed is a factor of a thousand error, and it is the mistake this capstone catches most often.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 5, 'At Sw 0.8 in the Ekene pore space, how is the total fluid compliance divided between the two phases?', '["The brine contributes 80 percent and the gas 20 percent, matching the saturations","The brine contributes 48.42 percent and the gas contributes the remainder","The two contribute roughly equally, because the saturations and the moduli offset each other","The brine contributes 7.6 percent and the gas contributes 92.4 percent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The brine term is 2.9654e-10 and the gas term is 3.5895e-9, so the 20 percent gas does 92 percent of the softening. Expecting the split to follow the saturations is the error of reading a harmonic average as a weighted average.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 6, 'What mixed fluid bulk modulus does the engine return for the capstone case of 80 percent brine and 20 percent gas?', '["2697.8113 MPa","257.3340919366766 MPa","55.7187 MPa","848.7933489234579 MPa"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded value is 257.3340919366766 MPa, with a tolerance of 0.5 MPa. The figure 2697.8113 MPa is the fully brine saturated case, 55.7187 MPa is gas alone, and 848.7933489234579 is the mixed density in kg/m3 rather than a modulus at all.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 7, 'What is the mixed fluid density at Sw 0.8, given brine at 1017.8249875 kg/m3 and gas at 172.66679461728904 kg/m3?', '["848.7933489234579 kg/m3","595.2458910586445 kg/m3","933.309168211729 kg/m3","1009.373405571173 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sum 0.8 x 1017.8249875 + 0.2 x 172.66679461728904 gives 848.7933489234579 kg/m3 exactly, which is what the engine returns. The other values are the mixed densities at Sw 0.50, Sw 0.90 and Sw 0.99.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 8, 'Moving from Sw 0.8 to Sw 0.9 changes the mixed fluid density by how much of the gap between the gas and brine densities?', '["By almost the whole gap, because the density follows the modulus","By nothing measurable, because density is insensitive to saturation","By one tenth of the gap, giving 933.309168211729 kg/m3","By one half of the gap, because the mixture sits halfway between the two cases"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Density mixing is linear, so a saturation step of 0.1 moves the density by a tenth of the gap and lands on the engine value of 933.309168211729 kg/m3. That straight line behaviour is what makes the modulus response so striking by comparison.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 9, 'Going from fully brine saturated to a water saturation of 0.99, what happens to the mixed fluid modulus and to the mixed fluid density?', '["Both fall by about a third","The modulus is unchanged while the density falls from 1017.8249875 to 1009.373405571173 kg/m3","The modulus falls from 2697.8113 to 1830.0363 MPa and the density falls by a similar proportion","The modulus falls from 2697.8113 to 1830.0363 MPa, a fall of about a third, while the density moves only from 1017.8249875 to 1009.373405571173 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One percent of gas removes about a third of the fluid stiffness while barely touching the density, for a saturation change no log could resolve. Assuming that a small gas fraction must be a small effect is the misconception this step is built to break.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 10, 'Replacing half of the pore volume, moving from Sw 0.50 to Sw 0.00, changes the mixed fluid modulus in what way?', '["From 469.8509 to 55.7187 MPa, which is a very large change","From 109.1823 to 55.7187 MPa, a small change compared with what the first one percent of gas did","From 257.3341 to 55.7187 MPa, the largest single step anywhere in the table","Not at all, because the fluid is already gas dominated at Sw 0.50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The curve has already collapsed by Sw 0.50, so half the pore volume changing hands moves the modulus only from 109.1823 to 55.7187 MPa. The first one percent of gas moved it from 2697.8113 to 1830.0363 MPa, a far larger step.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 11, 'What must Wood''s equation return at a water saturation of 0.0?', '["The gas properties unchanged, 55.7187 MPa and 172.66679461728904 kg/m3","The brine properties unchanged, 2697.8113 MPa and 1017.8249875 kg/m3","A modulus of zero, because there is no brine left to stiffen the pore","The capstone mix of 257.3340919366766 MPa, because the formula is calibrated to that case"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With the water saturation at zero the brine term drops out of the compliance sum and the mixture reduces to the single phase. Reducing correctly at both ends of the saturation range is a required property of any mixing law and a free check on an implementation.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 12, 'Given brine at 2697.8113 MPa and gas at 55.7187 MPa mixed at 80 percent brine, where should you expect the answer to sit before you calculate anything?', '["Near the brine value, because brine occupies four fifths of the pore","Exactly four fifths of the way from the gas value up to the brine value","Near the gas value, because a harmonic average is pulled toward its most compliant input","Above the brine value, because mixing two fluids stiffens the pore"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pore fluid takes on the character of its softest phase well before that phase reaches half the pore volume, and the result of 257.3340919366766 MPa sits down among the gas values. Expecting the answer near the abundant phase is the weighted average intuition, and it fails here.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 13, 'Why does a given saturation of gas carry so much more weight in the compliance sum than the same saturation of brine?', '["Because gas has the lower density, and density enters the compliance sum","Because the engine applies a correction factor to the gas term","Because gas saturation is defined as one minus the water saturation","Because brine is 48.42 times stiffer than the gas at these conditions, so the gas brings a compliance about fifty times larger into the sum"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Compliance is the reciprocal of modulus, so the ratio of the two moduli becomes the ratio of the two weights. That single ratio of 48.42 is what puts the gas term two orders of magnitude above the brine term at Sw 0.8.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 14, 'Why is seismic described as a good gas detector but a poor gas meter?', '["Because seismic resolution is too coarse to image a reservoir at all","Because the fluid modulus collapses in the first few percent of gas and then flattens, so an amplitude shows that gas is present without showing how much","Because gas saturation cannot be measured in a well either","Because the density change with saturation is too large to separate from the modulus change"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Almost all of the sensitivity is spent before the saturation reaches a level anyone would call a discovery. A sand at 20 percent gas gives 257.3341 MPa and a sand at 80 percent gas gives 69.2905 MPa, and both are so far below the brine value of 2697.8113 MPa that the two are hard to separate under ordinary uncertainty.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-mixing-the-pore-fluid', 15, 'By the capstone saturation of 20 percent gas, how far has the fluid modulus fallen from its fully brine saturated value?', '["By about a factor of ten, from 2697.8113 to 257.3341 MPa","By about 20 percent, matching the gas saturation","By about a third, the same as the first one percent of gas achieved","It has risen, because the pore now contains two phases"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table runs from 2697.8113 MPa at Sw 1.00 to 257.3341 MPa at Sw 0.80, which is a fall of about a factor of ten. Expecting the fall to track the gas saturation is the weighted average intuition once more.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 1, 'Brine at 25 MPa and 35,000 ppm is computed at 20, 40, 60, 80 and 100 degC. Which statement describes how its bulk modulus behaves across that range?', '["It falls steadily throughout, because warming always softens a liquid.","It rises steadily throughout, because warming always stiffens a liquid.","It peaks between 60 and 80 degC and falls away on both sides of that peak.","It stays fixed, because bulk modulus depends on pressure and not on temperature."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brine bulk modulus is not monotonic in temperature. It rises to 2.697811 GPa at 60 degC, then falls to 2.689136 GPa at 80 degC and 2.629247 GPa at 100 degC. The first option is the classic ''warmer means softer'' intuition, which is the very trend this table breaks.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 2, 'Brine at 25 MPa and 35,000 ppm gives a bulk modulus of 2.511437 GPa at 20 degC, 2.642625 GPa at 40 degC, 2.697811 GPa at 60 degC, 2.689136 GPa at 80 degC and 2.629247 GPa at 100 degC. Which temperature gives the stiffest brine?', '["20 degC","60 degC","80 degC","100 degC"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The largest value in the column is 2.697811 GPa at 60 degC. Picking 20 degC assumes the modulus falls with temperature and picking 100 degC assumes it rises, and both assume a monotonic trend that this brine does not follow.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 3, 'Across the same temperature sweep the brine velocity reads 1559.8600 m/s at 20 degC, 1604.8488 at 40 degC, 1628.0556 at 60 degC, 1633.6850 at 80 degC and 1625.2726 at 100 degC. Which is the fastest brine and where does it occur?', '["1628.0556 m/s at 60 degC, the same row as the stiffest brine","1559.8600 m/s at 20 degC, the coldest row","1625.2726 m/s at 100 degC, the hottest row","1633.6850 m/s at 80 degC, one row past the stiffest brine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The velocity peak sits at 80 degC, one row later than the bulk modulus peak at 60 degC. Assuming the two peaks coincide is the common error, and the first option states exactly that assumption.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 4, 'Why does the brine velocity keep rising from 60 degC to 80 degC even though the bulk modulus has already begun to fall?', '["Velocity is the square root of K over rho, and density is still falling from 1017.8250 to 1007.5722 kg/m3, which buys more speed than the loss of stiffness costs.","The engine rounds velocity differently from bulk modulus.","Salinity rises with temperature, which speeds the wave up.","Velocity depends only on density, so the bulk modulus is irrelevant."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Velocity carries both the stiffness and the density, so it inherits the shape of each. The lighter brine at 80 degC more than covers the small drop from 2.697811 to 2.689136 GPa.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 5, 'Over the same 20 to 100 degC sweep at 25 MPa and 35,000 ppm, how does brine density behave?', '["It peaks between 60 and 80 degC, in step with the bulk modulus.","It falls at every step, from 1032.1697 kg/m3 to 995.3571 kg/m3.","It rises at every step, from 995.3571 kg/m3 to 1032.1697 kg/m3.","It is unchanged, because density is set by salinity alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Density is the well behaved half of the pair. It falls monotonically across the whole range, which is why it cannot be used to diagnose the non-monotonic behaviour of the bulk modulus.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 6, 'A 0.6 gravity gas at 60 degC gives a density of 69.5252 kg/m3 and a bulk modulus of 17.7074 MPa at 10 MPa, and 237.8996 kg/m3 with 111.3929 MPa at 40 MPa. What is the gas bulk modulus at 40 MPa?', '["111.3929 MPa","17.7074 MPa","55.7187 MPa","237.8996 MPa"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bulk modulus at 40 MPa is 111.3929 MPa. Choosing 237.8996 reads the density column instead of the stiffness column, which is easy to do when both are quoted side by side.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 7, 'A 0.6 gravity gas at 60 degC is taken from a pore pressure of 10 MPa to one of 40 MPa. What happens to its two properties?', '["The density rises while the bulk modulus falls.","The bulk modulus rises while the density falls.","Neither moves much, because a gas is already very compressible.","Both rise, the density from 69.5252 to 237.8996 kg/m3 and the bulk modulus from 17.7074 to 111.3929 MPa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Squeezing a gas packs more molecules into the same space, so it gets heavier and harder to compress at once. Both columns climb, and the stiffness climbs the harder of the two.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 8, 'Which pressure belongs in a Batzle and Wang fluid calculation for a reservoir sand?', '["The pore pressure, which is the pressure the fluid itself is under.","The overburden, which is the weight of the rock column above.","The effective stress, which is the overburden minus the pore pressure.","The difference between the overburden and the fracture pressure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fluid calculation takes the pressure acting on the fluid, which is the pore pressure and is 25 MPa for the Ekene sand. Effective stress is what the rock frame feels and belongs to the frame work at the tier above.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 9, 'Holding 60 degC and 25 MPa fixed, brine salinity is raised from 0 wt% to 20 wt%. What happens?', '["The density falls and the bulk modulus rises.","Both fall, the density from 1138.4297 to 994.0097 kg/m3 and the bulk modulus from 3.611999 to 2.534420 GPa.","Both rise, the density from 994.0097 to 1138.4297 kg/m3 and the bulk modulus from 2.534420 to 3.611999 GPa.","The density rises and the bulk modulus is unchanged."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dissolved salt adds mass and pulls the surrounding water into a tighter arrangement, so it raises density and stiffness together. Unlike temperature, salinity moves both properties monotonically.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 10, 'Fresh brine at 60 degC and 25 MPa gives a bulk modulus of 2.534420 GPa, while Ekene brine at 20 degC gives 2.511437 GPa. What does that near coincidence warn you about?', '["That the engine cannot distinguish salinity from temperature.","That fresh brine and cold brine are physically the same fluid.","That a salinity error and a temperature error can land on almost the same modulus, so checking the output alone will not tell them apart.","That salinity has no real effect once the temperature is known."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two quite different input mistakes produce nearly the same answer, so an output that looks plausible is not evidence that the inputs were right. The defence is to check the stated conditions rather than the computed value.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 11, 'The Ekene brine salinity is quoted as 35,000 ppm. How does that appear as a weight fraction and as a weight percent?', '["0.0035 weight fraction, which is 0.35 wt%","0.35 weight fraction, which is 35 wt%","0.035 weight fraction, which is 35 wt%","0.035 weight fraction, which is 3.5 wt%"]'::jsonb, 3, '35,000 ppm, a weight fraction of 0.035 and 3.5 wt% are the same statement in three forms, and the engine takes the weight fraction. Field data arrives in all three, so moving between them has to be automatic.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 12, 'Holding 60 degC and 25 MPa fixed, gas gravity is raised from 0.6 to 1.0. What happens to the gas?', '["Both properties rise, the density from 172.6668 to 337.4248 kg/m3 and the bulk modulus from 55.7187 to 119.8581 MPa.","Both properties fall, because a richer gas is more compressible.","The density rises to 337.4248 kg/m3 while the bulk modulus stays at 55.7187 MPa.","The bulk modulus rises to 119.8581 MPa while the density falls to 172.6668 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A higher gravity describes a gas carrying more of the heavier components, which is both heavier and stiffer at the same conditions. That makes a rich gas a weaker contrast against brine than a dry gas at the same depth.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 13, 'An oil property set measured on a stock tank sample stands at GOR 0 and gives a density of 820.9856 kg/m3. The capstone grades the live-oil density as 777.0630099023522 kg/m3 with a tolerance of 0.5 kg/m3. What happens if the stock tank value is used?', '["It passes, because the two values are within a few percent of each other.","It fails the 0.5 kg/m3 tolerance by a wide margin, and the stiffness is wrong too, at 1.475341 GPa in place of 1.142795 GPa.","It passes as long as the sample source is recorded alongside it.","It affects the density only, because dissolved gas does not change the bulk modulus."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock tank sample loses its dissolved gas on the way to surface, so it measures a fluid that does not exist in the reservoir. The measurement is accurate and the fluid is the wrong one, which is the specific failure this module exists to prevent.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 14, 'At 60 degC and 25 MPa with a stock tank density of 0.85 g/cc, the oil is computed at GOR 0, 50 and 150 L/L. What does dissolved gas do to the oil?', '["It makes the oil heavier and stiffer.","It makes the oil lighter and stiffer.","It makes the oil lighter and much softer, from 820.9856 to 693.8631 kg/m3 and from 1.475341 to 0.715855 GPa.","It makes the oil heavier and softer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gas going into solution lightens the oil and softens it at the same time, and the stiffness falls the harder of the two. The velocity carries both effects and falls from 1340.5357 to 1015.7240 m/s.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-how-conditions-change-the-answer', 15, 'What does the fluid explorer panel embedded in this module let you change?', '["The temperature, so you can reproduce the brine against temperature table.","The pore pressure, so you can reproduce the gas against pressure table.","The salinity and the gas gravity, so you can reproduce the composition tables.","The water saturation, with the conditions held at 60 degC and 25 MPa, so you can re-read the fluids, the frame and the Wood mix."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The panel varies saturation only and holds the Ekene conditions fixed. Assuming it varies temperature or pressure is a common misreading, and the condition tables in this module are read from the lesson text instead.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 1, 'In the seven step Beginner workflow, which step produces no number of its own and yet controls every number that follows it?', '["Stating the conditions, which for the Ekene sand are 60 degC, 25 MPa, salinity 0.035, gas gravity 0.6 and GOR 50 L/L.","Computing the brine, because every other fluid is compared against it.","Mixing the mineral frame, because the frame is the stiffest thing in the problem.","Mixing the pore fluid with Wood''s equation, because it is the last step before the hand over."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each fluid is evaluated at the stated conditions, so a condition typed wrongly is carried silently into every value downstream. No later step in the chain can detect it.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 2, 'A colleague sends you a single line that reads ''brine bulk modulus 2.6978112899395996 GPa'' and nothing else. What is missing, and why does it matter?', '["Nothing is missing, because the value is quoted to enough digits to be unambiguous.","The conditions it was computed at, since the same brine gives 2.511437 GPa at 20 degC and 2.629247 GPa at 100 degC.","The unit, since a bulk modulus can be quoted in either GPa or MPa.","The water saturation, since a brine modulus depends on how much gas is in the pore space."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first QC check is that every fluid is quoted with its conditions, because the number alone will not tell you which temperature, pressure or salinity it belongs to. The unit is already present in this line, and a single phase brine modulus does not depend on saturation.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 3, 'Of the six graded capstone fields, which pair is graded in MPa?', '["The brine bulk modulus and the frame bulk modulus.","The brine bulk modulus and the gas bulk modulus.","The gas bulk modulus and the Wood mixed-fluid modulus.","The frame bulk modulus and the Wood mixed-fluid modulus."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gas bulk modulus is graded as 55.71865290286663 MPa and the Wood mixed fluid as 257.3340919366766 MPa, while brine and frame bulk moduli are graded in GPa. Mixing those two habits up is the single biggest source of lost marks on this capstone.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 4, 'Which of the six graded capstone fields carries the tightest tolerance, and what is it?', '["The Wood mixed-fluid modulus, at 0.5 MPa.","The gas bulk modulus, at 0.1 MPa.","The frame bulk modulus, at 0.05 GPa.","The brine bulk modulus, at 0.005 GPa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The brine bulk modulus is graded to 0.005 GPa, the narrowest window on the paper. Assuming a looser tolerance applies is how a value that was read carelessly gets submitted.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 5, 'A learner types 2697.8113 into the brine bulk modulus field. What has gone wrong?', '["They have entered the value in MPa in a field graded in GPa, which wants 2.6978112899395996 GPa.","Nothing, because that is the correct brine bulk modulus at the Ekene conditions.","They have read the mixed fluid line instead of the brine line.","They have left the saturation slider at the wrong value."]'::jsonb, 0, '2697.8113 MPa and 2.6978112899395996 GPa are the same stiffness in different units, and only the GPa form is inside the 0.005 GPa window. This is the unit trap running in the direction of a value that looks like a stress.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 6, 'At Sw 0.8 the two end members are the gas at 55.7187 MPa and the brine at 2697.8113 MPa, and the Wood mixed fluid comes out at 257.3340919366766 MPa. What does that position tell you?', '["That the brine dominates the mix, since it is 80 percent of the pore fluid.","That the mix sits far down at the soft gas end, which is what a harmonic average has to do.","That the gas fraction must have been entered as 0.8 rather than 0.2.","That the mix has been averaged linearly between the two end members."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Wood''s equation averages compliances, so the softest phase dominates whatever its volume fraction. The brine term contributes 7.6 percent of the total compliance and the gas term 92.4 percent, so 20 percent gas does 92 percent of the softening.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 7, 'Which quantity in the pore fluid mix behaves exactly linearly, so that the QC check on it is an equality rather than a judgement?', '["The bulk modulus, since Wood''s equation is a weighted average.","The velocity, since it is the square root of stiffness over density.","The density, since 0.8 x 1017.8249875 + 0.2 x 172.66679461728904 = 848.7933489234579 kg/m3.","The shear modulus, since fluids carry no shear stiffness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Density mixes linearly and the modulus mixes harmonically, so the two checks cover each other. If the linear sum does not reproduce the engine value, the saturations you typed are not the ones the engine used.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 8, 'The 70/30 quartz and clay frame gives a Reuss bulk modulus of 29.868801 GPa, a Voigt bulk modulus of 31.890000 GPa and a Voigt Reuss Hill value of 30.87940062475596 GPa. Which ordering must always hold?', '["Voigt below VRH below Reuss.","VRH above both bounds.","VRH below both bounds.","Reuss below VRH below Voigt."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A Voigt Reuss Hill value is the mean of a lower and an upper bound, so it has to lie between them. This check needs no tolerance argument, since a VRH value outside its own bounds is arithmetically impossible.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 9, 'What must the density of the 70/30 quartz and clay frame be, and why is that an exact check rather than an approximate one?', '["2629 kg/m3, because density mixes exactly linearly as 0.7 x 2650 + 0.3 x 2580.","2650 kg/m3, because quartz is the dominant mineral.","2580 kg/m3, because the clay sets the lower bound.","Somewhere between 2580 and 2650 kg/m3, because density has bounds in the same way the moduli do."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Density needs no Voigt or Reuss bounds because the mixing is exact, so the engine must return 2629 kg/m3. An exact check fails loudly, and a frame density that is not 2629 means the mineral fractions are not 70 and 30.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 10, 'For the 70/30 quartz and clay frame the two bulk modulus bounds sit about 6.8 percent apart while the two shear bounds sit nearly a factor of two apart. What follows from that?', '["Both VRH values are equally reliable, since both are averages of bounds.","The VRH bulk modulus is a well constrained claim about the rock while the VRH shear modulus is a much weaker one.","The VRH shear modulus is the better constrained of the two, since its bounds are further apart.","The mineral fractions must have been entered wrongly, since the two spreads should match."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A narrow spread means the answer barely depends on the rock geometry, and a wide one means it depends on it heavily. Quartz is about 6.5 times stiffer in shear than clay, which is why the shear bounds separate so far.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 11, 'The fluid explorer panel is embedded in the quality control lesson and in the capstone walkthrough. What does it let you vary?', '["The temperature and the pore pressure, so you can check the condition tables.","The mineral fractions, so you can check the Voigt and Reuss bounds.","The water saturation, with the conditions fixed at 60 degC and 25 MPa.","The salinity and the gas gravity, so you can check the composition tables."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The panel varies saturation only. Expecting it to move the conditions is a common misreading, and it is also why the harmonic mixing check can be watched staying true while the numbers change.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 12, 'The panel displays a frame shear modulus of 25.25472176759411 GPa beside a frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa. Which of the two does the Beginner capstone grade?', '["Both, since the frame is described by the pair together.","The shear modulus, since it is the fluid blind property.","Neither, since the frame is only an intermediate result.","The bulk modulus only, in GPa with a tolerance of 0.05."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Only the bulk modulus is graded, as the field labelled Frame K, VRH 70/30 quartz/clay. The shear value sits directly beside it on the panel and is the number most often entered in the wrong box at this tier.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 13, 'The Wood field is graded at Sw 0.8. A learner leaves the saturation slider at Sw 0.9, reads 469.8509 MPa and submits it. What is the outcome?', '["It falls outside the tolerance, since the graded value is 257.3340919366766 MPa with a window of 0.5 MPa.","It passes, since 0.9 and 0.8 are close enough for a 0.5 MPa tolerance.","It passes, since the Wood mixed fluid does not depend on saturation.","It passes once the value is converted from MPa to GPa."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Wood modulus is strongly saturation dependent, reading 109.1823 MPa at Sw 0.50 and 469.8509 MPa at Sw 0.90. A slider left in the wrong place gives a number that looks entirely reasonable and is far outside a 0.5 MPa window.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 14, 'The Professional tier reports a dry frame bulk modulus of 7.350343061720982 GPa for the Ekene sand, while this tier computes a mineral frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa. Why is the dry frame so much smaller?', '["Because the two values are quoted in different units.","Because the mineral value describes solid grains welded into a block, while the dry frame describes a real rock that is a quarter pore space with grains touching only at contacts.","Because the dry frame is computed with a different mineral mixture.","Because the dry frame includes the brine and the mineral frame does not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mineral frame and the dry frame are different objects. Porosity of 0.25 and the grain contacts of a real rock are what separate them, and holding the two apart is most of the work at the tier above.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-rock-physics-workflow', 15, 'As a forward pointer, what does the Expert tier report about the Ekene sand when brine is substituted for gas, and what tuning thickness does it pick at 25 Hz?', '["The sand stays in the same AVO class and the tuning thickness is 16 ms.","The sand flips from class III under brine to class I under gas, with 16 ms of tuning.","The sand flips from class I under brine to class III under gas, with 16 ms of tuning.","The sand flips from class II to class IV, with 25 ms of tuning."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fluid substitution flips the sand from class I to class III, which is a qualitative change in what an interpreter sees on a gather rather than a small numerical shift. The wedge model picks 16 ms of tuning at 25 Hz.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A report quotes a reservoir brine bulk modulus of 2.534420 GPa and gives no other information. Why can that number not be used?', '["Several different reservoirs give a value close to it, including fresh water at 60 degC and 25 MPa and the Ekene brine at 0.035 salinity but 20 degC, so the number does not identify its own case.","The value is too low to be a brine, so it must be a reporting error.","Bulk modulus is not a quantity a seismic wave responds to, so a density should have been quoted instead.","The value is fine to use, because brine properties vary little between reservoirs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fresh water at 60 degC and 25 MPa gives exactly 2.534420 GPa, and the Ekene brine at its full salinity but 20 degC gives 2.511437 GPa, so two very different reservoirs sit behind similar numbers. A fluid property without its conditions cannot be attached to a reservoir or checked against anything.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Which ordering of the three Ekene fluid moduli, from stiffest to softest, is stated correctly?', '["Gas 55.71865290286663 MPa, brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, live oil 1.1427945726905131 GPa.","Brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, live oil 1.1427945726905131 GPa, gas 55.71865290286663 MPa.","Live oil 1.1427945726905131 GPa, brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, gas 55.71865290286663 MPa.","Brine 2.6978112899395996 GPa, gas 55.71865290286663 MPa, live oil 1.1427945726905131 GPa."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Brine is the stiffest, live oil is next, and gas is far below both. Every wrong option ranks the gas as though its MPa figure could be compared directly against a GPa figure, which is the unit error this course exists to prevent.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'The pore fluid in a sand is changed from brine to gas. What happens to the compressional and shear velocities of the rock, and why?', '["Both velocities fall sharply, because the rock has become both lighter and softer.","The shear velocity falls sharply while the compressional velocity is almost unchanged.","The compressional velocity falls sharply while the shear velocity moves only slightly, because the fluid contributes to the bulk modulus and the density but adds no shear stiffness.","Neither velocity changes, because the mineral frame is what carries the wave."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every fluid has a shear modulus of zero, so the shear velocity moves only through the density in its denominator, and a lighter rock can even push it up slightly. The compressional velocity feels both the collapse in fluid modulus and the drop in density, and the modulus effect wins.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The Ekene sand is re-evaluated at 40 MPa instead of 25 MPa, with everything else held. Which statement is correct?', '["All six capstone numbers move, because pressure acts on the whole rock.","Only the gas numbers move, because gas is the only compressible fluid in the pore space.","None of the six move, because the capstone is defined at a fixed set of conditions.","The five fluid derived capstone numbers all move, while the frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa is untouched."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The frame comes from the mineral constants and the 70 to 30 quartz to clay split, neither of which depends on pressure at this tier. The brine, gas and oil values all respond, and the gas responds most steeply, going from 55.7187 MPa at 25 MPa to 111.3929 MPa at 40 MPa.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'For the 70 to 30 quartz to clay frame, the two bulk modulus bounds sit about 6.8 percent apart while the two shear modulus bounds sit nearly a factor of two apart. What does that difference license you to claim?', '["The mixed bulk modulus is well constrained whatever the rock geometry, while the mixed shear modulus is a much weaker claim about the same rock.","Both moduli are equally well constrained, because both come from the same lithology split.","The shear modulus is the better constrained of the two, because quartz and clay differ less in shear.","Neither modulus can be quoted at all, because the two bounds do not coincide."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The exact bound ratios are 1.067669 for bulk modulus and 1.981765 for shear modulus, and the wide shear spread follows from quartz being about 6.5 times stiffer in shear than clay, at 45.0 GPa against 6.9 GPa. A narrow spread means the answer barely depends on how the minerals are arranged, and a wide one means it depends a great deal.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Compare a dry gas of gravity 0.6 at 10 MPa and 60 degC with a wetter gas of gravity 1.0 at 25 MPa and 60 degC. Which has the lower bulk modulus, and what does that mean for the seismic fluid effect?', '["The wetter gas at 25 MPa, at 119.8581 MPa against 17.7074 MPa, so it gives the stronger effect.","The dry shallow gas, at 17.7074 MPa against 119.8581 MPa, so it contrasts more strongly with the brine it replaces and gives the stronger effect.","They are the same, because the higher gravity and the higher pressure cancel each other out.","Neither can be compared, because a gas modulus depends only on temperature."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both a higher pressure and a heavier gravity stiffen a gas, so the two act in the same direction here and the wetter gas at 25 MPa is far stiffer than the dry gas at 10 MPa. A softer gas leaves a wider gap against the brine it replaces and therefore a stronger fluid effect.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'An interpreter models the Ekene sand using the dead oil density of 820.9856 kg/m3 in place of the live oil density of 777.0630099023522 kg/m3. What is the effect?', '["The modelled rock is lighter than the real one, because a dead oil has lost its gas.","There is no effect, because a fluid density does not reach the impedance of the rock.","The modelled rock is heavier than the real one, so its impedance is overstated.","The effect is confined to the shear velocity, since density does not enter compressional velocity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dissolved gas makes oil lighter, so the dead value of 820.9856 kg/m3 sits above the live value of 777.0630099023522 kg/m3 and putting it in the pore space adds mass the reservoir does not have. Density enters both velocities and the impedance, so the error is not confined to one wave.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'What compressional velocity does this course quote for the Ekene gas?', '["1628.0555893189182 m/s, the same value it returns for the brine.","1212.7072294996883 m/s, since gas and live oil are both hydrocarbons.","A value below 500 m/s, since gas is the softest fluid in the course.","None, because the engine does not return a compressional velocity for gas."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns a density and a bulk modulus for gas and nothing else, so no gas velocity appears anywhere in the course. Borrowing another fluid''s velocity or inventing a plausible one is the habit that the discipline about sourcing numbers is meant to prevent.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'Why is discriminating oil from brine on seismic harder than discriminating gas from brine?', '["The step from brine at 2.6978112899395996 GPa to live oil at 1.1427945726905131 GPa stays within the same order of magnitude, while the step to gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa crosses more than one.","Oil and brine have identical densities, so no contrast exists between them at all.","Oil has a shear modulus while brine does not, and shear effects are hard to record.","Oil is only ever found below the contact, where seismic resolution is poorer."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both a brine to oil swap and a brine to gas swap change the fluid modulus and the density, but the oil case is a modest change while the gas case is a collapse of nearly fifty times in stiffness. No fluid in this course has a shear modulus, oil included.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'How does the Ekene mineral frame compare with the pore fluids in stiffness?', '["The frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa is close to the brine value of 2.6978112899395996 GPa.","The frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa is far above every fluid in the course, including the gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa.","The frame is softer than the brine, which is why the fluids control the rock response.","The frame and the gas are on the same scale, since one is quoted near 30 and the other near 55."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rock is stiff and the fluids are soft, and the last option is the unit trap, since 55.71865290286663 is in MPa while 30.87940062475596 is in GPa. How much the soft fluid can move the stiff frame is the question the Professional tier answers.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'In the brine temperature table at 25 MPa and 0.035 salinity, which column changes in one direction throughout the range from 20 to 100 degC?', '["Bulk modulus, which rises throughout, from 2.511437 GPa to 2.629247 GPa.","Compressional velocity, which rises throughout, from 1559.8600 m/s to 1625.2726 m/s.","Density, which falls throughout, from 1032.1697 kg/m3 to 995.3571 kg/m3.","Every column in the table changes in one direction over this range."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Density falls steadily as the brine expands with heat, while the bulk modulus peaks between 60 and 80 degC and the velocity reaches its highest listed value of 1633.6850 m/s at 80 degC before falling back. Assuming that every property varies monotonically with temperature is the error this table corrects.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'What does the Associate tier of this course deliberately not do, and why?', '["It does not compute gas properties, because gas is held back to the Professional tier.","It does not use engine output, because every value is taken from published tables.","It does not mix minerals, because the frame belongs to the Advanced tier.","It does not run Gassmann fluid substitution, because substitution performed on fluid and frame values that are not understood produces confident numbers that are wrong in ways you cannot see."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tier builds the ingredients, which are the three fluids, the mineral frame and the mixed pore fluid, and holds substitution back to the Professional tier. Every argument the higher tiers make about a bright amplitude rests on the fluid moduli built here.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Which property of a mineral mixture combines exactly linearly with the volume fractions, with no bounds and no ambiguity?', '["Density, since 0.7 times 2650 plus 0.3 times 2580 gives the 2629 kg/m3 the engine returns.","Bulk modulus, since it is the mineral bulk moduli weighted by their fractions.","Shear modulus, since the shear response of a mixture is the mean of its parts.","Compressional velocity, since it is a weighted mean of the mineral velocities."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Density is a mass per unit volume and adds directly, so it needs no upper and lower bound. The moduli depend on how the minerals are arranged in the rock, which is why they arrive as a pair of bounds with an average between them.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'A report states that the reservoir gas has a bulk modulus of 17.7074 MPa and that the reservoir is at 60 degC and 25 MPa. What should you conclude?', '["The numbers are consistent, since a gas modulus depends on temperature rather than pressure.","The numbers are inconsistent, because a 0.6 gravity gas at 60 degC and 25 MPa gives 55.7187 MPa, while 17.7074 MPa is the value at 10 MPa.","The numbers are consistent, since 17.7074 MPa and 55.7187 MPa agree once both are converted to GPa.","The report is fine, because a gas modulus is a table constant and does not depend on conditions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A gas bulk modulus is a steep function of pressure, so quoting a value from 10 MPa beside a stated reservoir pressure of 25 MPa is a contradiction on the face of the report. Checking a fluid property against the conditions printed next to it is the habit this course is trying to build.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'The same harmonic average appears twice in this course, once as the lower bound for the mineral frame and once as the answer for the pore fluid. What accounts for the difference in status?', '["Fluids carry no shear stress and equalise pressure when well mixed, so equal stress is what actually happens, while in a mineral mixture it is only one of many possible geometries","The mineral version uses volume fractions while the fluid version uses saturations, and saturations are exact","Wood''s equation includes a correction term that the Reuss bound lacks","The fluid moduli are much smaller, and harmonic averaging becomes exact for small moduli"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The algebra is identical in the two cases, so the difference in status comes entirely from the physics of the mixture. A well mixed pore fluid genuinely sits at the equal stress limit, while a rock only might.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Which pair of quantities in the Beginner workflow mixes exactly, with no bounds and no assumption about how the components are arranged?', '["The frame bulk modulus and the mixed fluid bulk modulus","The frame shear modulus and the mixed fluid density","The frame density of 2629 kg/m3 and the mixed fluid density of 848.7933489234579 kg/m3","The Voigt bound and the Reuss bound, since both are exact limits"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mass and volume add, so both densities are volume weighted sums that no geometry can disturb. Every modulus in the workflow depends either on the arrangement of the components or on the reciprocal weighting that arrangement implies.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Four values are quoted to full engine precision: the frame density of 2629 kg/m3, the frame shear modulus of 25.25472176759411 GPa, the frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa and the mixed fluid density of 848.7933489234579 kg/m3. Which one carries the least real precision?', '["The frame density, because mineral densities vary between samples","The frame shear modulus, because its bounds of 16.939444 and 33.570000 GPa sit nearly a factor of two apart","The frame bulk modulus, because it is a midpoint of two bounds rather than a measurement","The mixed fluid density, because it depends on a saturation you cannot measure from seismic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Printed digits are not evidence of precision. The shear modulus is the midpoint of a range spanning nearly a factor of two, while the bulk modulus midpoint sits inside a spread of about 6.8 percent and both densities are exact given their inputs.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Which reported value for a rock modelled as 70 percent quartz and 30 percent clay with brine and gas in its pores should be rejected on sight?', '["A frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa","A mixed fluid modulus of 257.3340919366766 MPa at Sw 0.8","A frame density of 2629 kg/m3","A frame bulk modulus of 33.570000 GPa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The bulk modulus of this frame has to lie between the Reuss bound of 29.868801 GPa and the Voigt bound of 31.890000 GPa, so 33.570000 GPa is impossible. That figure is the Voigt bound for the shear modulus, and reading across from the wrong column is the error being tested.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'You hold a frame bulk modulus with bounds about 6.8 percent apart, a frame shear modulus with bounds nearly a factor of two apart, and a pore fluid whose modulus is controlled by a gas saturation that seismic cannot resolve. Where is a constraint worth paying for?', '["The frame bulk modulus, because it enters every calculation downstream","The mineral densities, because they are the only exact inputs in the workflow","The frame shear modulus and the gas saturation, because those are the inputs whose plausible ranges are wide enough to change a decision","The saturation alone, because a modulus that comes from an averaging rule can never be improved by measurement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Money goes where the range is wide and the decision is sensitive, which points to shear data such as core or a shear log, and to saturation data from a well. The bulk modulus is already pinned by its narrow bounds and the densities are exact given their inputs.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Order these three from smallest to largest: the gas modulus at reservoir conditions, the mixed fluid modulus at Sw 0.8, and the fully brine saturated fluid modulus.', '["55.7187 MPa, then 257.3340919366766 MPa, then 2697.8113 MPa","55.7187 MPa, then 2697.8113 MPa, then 257.3340919366766 MPa","257.3340919366766 MPa, then 55.7187 MPa, then 2697.8113 MPa","2697.8113 MPa, then 257.3340919366766 MPa, then 55.7187 MPa"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A harmonic average has to lie between its inputs, so the mix sits between the gas and brine values, and it sits far closer to the gas end. All three are quoted in MPa here, since the brine modulus is reported as 2.6978112899395996 GPa elsewhere in the course.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'What do the Reuss mineral bound and the Wood fluid mix have in common?', '["Both are arithmetic averages of their components","Both assume that every component suffers the same strain","Both are upper limits on the modulus of the mixture","Both add compliances, so both are dominated by the most compliant component present"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reciprocal weighting means the softest component brings the largest term into the sum in each case. That is why 30 percent clay pulls the shear bound down to 16.939444 GPa and why 20 percent gas supplies 92.4 percent of the fluid compliance.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'The frame bulk modulus is reported in GPa and the mixed fluid modulus in MPa. Which statement about those two values is correct?', '["They can be compared as printed, since both are bulk moduli of the same rock","The frame value of 30.87940062475596 GPa is far larger than the mixed fluid value of 257.3340919366766 MPa, and the units must be brought together before any comparison","The mixed fluid value is the larger of the two, since 257.3340919366766 exceeds 30.87940062475596","The two cannot be compared at all, because one describes a solid and the other a fluid"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The solid frame is orders of magnitude stiffer than the pore fluid, and only a common unit makes that visible. Reading 257.3340919366766 as the larger number is the same GPa against MPa slip that ruins a Wood compliance sum.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Of the VRH average and Wood''s equation, which is a convention and which is a physical result?', '["Both are physical results derived from the elasticity of mixtures","Both are conventions with no physical content behind them","VRH is a pragmatic choice of a midpoint inside a range, while Wood is the physical result for a well mixed pore fluid","VRH is the physical result while Wood is a convention adopted for convenience"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing makes the midpoint of two limiting geometries the true modulus of a rock, so VRH is a defensible choice rather than a derivation. Wood follows from pressure equalisation in a fluid that cannot support shear, which is physics for as long as the saturation stays uniform.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Two separate arguments were given for why a seismic amplitude cannot be inverted to a gas saturation. What are they?', '["The modulus curve flattens once any gas is present, and the distribution of that gas between uniform and patchy is unobservable while changing the stiffness","The density change is too large to model, and the frame shear modulus is poorly constrained","Seismic resolution is too coarse, and logs cannot measure saturation either","The brine modulus varies with temperature, and the gas modulus varies with pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The flat part of the saturation curve means very different saturations produce similar amplitudes, and patchy saturation shifts the whole response toward the stiff side at the same saturation. Both limits apply at once, and both belong in the answer when someone asks for a number.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'What general rule about mixture estimates does the contrast between the bulk and shear bounds of this frame illustrate?', '["That harmonic averages are always less reliable than arithmetic averages","That the reliability of an estimate improves as the number of components rises","That estimates for stiff minerals are inherently more reliable than estimates for soft ones","That the reliability of a mixture estimate is set by how far apart the end members sit, rather than by the sophistication of the mixing law"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One averaging rule gave a confident bulk modulus and a weak shear modulus for the same rock, and the whole difference is in the inputs. A wide bound spread is a signal to go and look at what the end members are.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Between a water saturation of 1.00 and 0.99 the fluid modulus falls from 2697.8113 to 1830.0363 MPa. What is the comparable statement about the frame over the full range of possible mineral arrangements?', '["The frame bulk modulus also falls by about a third","The frame bulk modulus can only move between 29.868801 and 31.890000 GPa, so the fluid is by far the more sensitive term","The frame bulk modulus is unaffected by anything, so it need not be reported","The frame shear modulus falls to zero as soon as gas is present"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One percent of gas moves the fluid modulus by about a third, while every possible arrangement of the same minerals moves the frame bulk modulus by about 6.8 percent. Saturation is the fast moving term in this workflow and the frame bulk modulus is the stable one.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'How should the two frame moduli and the mixed fluid modulus be reported to a colleague who will use them downstream?', '["With the bounds attached to the frame moduli, and with the saturation and the uniform mixing assumption stated alongside the fluid modulus","As three numbers at full engine precision, since the engine is the reference","As three rounded numbers with no further context, to keep the report readable","With the frame moduli rounded and the fluid modulus at full precision, since only the fluid modulus is graded"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bounds are the honest part of a VRH value, and the saturation and mixing assumption are the honest part of a Wood value. Full precision on its own reads as a measurement and hides the width of the range underneath.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'In this workflow, 30 percent clay and 20 percent gas are both minority components. How do their effects compare?', '["Both shift their mixture by roughly their own fraction, as minority components generally do","Neither has much effect, because each mixture is dominated by its majority phase","The clay moves the frame bulk modulus by only about 6.8 percent across the full range of arrangements, while the gas supplies 92.4 percent of the fluid compliance","The clay controls the frame bulk modulus completely, while the gas has only a modest effect on the fluid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'How much a minority component matters depends on how different it is from the majority, and gas differs from brine by a factor of 48.42 in stiffness while quartz and clay are within a factor of two in bulk modulus. The same 30 percent clay does open the shear bounds to nearly a factor of two, which makes the point again from the other direction.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'A fluid modulus computed at surface conditions is handed over without its conditions attached and used further down the workflow. Where does the error enter, and what will catch it later?', '["It enters at the fluid step, and nothing later catches it, because every step after it is arithmetic on whatever input it was given.","It enters at the frame step, and the Voigt and Reuss bound check catches it.","It enters at the mixing step, and the linear density check catches it.","It enters at the reporting step, and the capstone tolerances catch it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fluid step is early and cheap to get right, and it is the last point at which a conditions error is still visible. The bound check and the density check test internal consistency, so both pass happily on a fluid computed at the wrong temperature.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Wood''s equation gives a mixed fluid modulus of 2697.8113 MPa at Sw 1.00 and 1830.0363 MPa at Sw 0.99. What is the significance of that pair?', '["It shows the mix responds only once the gas fraction is substantial.","It shows about a third of the fluid stiffness is lost for a saturation change no log could resolve.","It shows the density is what carries the gas signal at low gas fractions.","It shows the harmonic average behaves linearly near full brine saturation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first one percent of gas does a disproportionate share of the softening, and by 20 percent gas the modulus is down by a factor of ten. Density over the same range barely moves, which is the opposite of the third option.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Which dial moved the brine bulk modulus further in this course: the full temperature sweep from 20 to 100 degC, or the salinity sweep from 0 to 20 wt%?', '["Temperature, which ran the modulus from 2.511437 to 2.697811 GPa.","They moved it by the same amount, which is why both must be quoted.","Salinity, which ran the modulus from 2.534420 to 3.611999 GPa.","Neither moved it by more than the 0.005 GPa capstone tolerance."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The composition dial moved the answer further than the state variable did, so composition is not a second order correction sitting on top of temperature and pressure. Both ranges are far larger than the 0.005 GPa tolerance, which rules out the last option.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Two identical gas accumulations sit at different depths, one at a pore pressure of 10 MPa and one at 40 MPa. Why is the deeper one a weaker seismic target?', '["Because the gas density falls with depth, so there is less mass contrast against the brine.","Because the brine softens with depth, so the two fluids converge from the brine side.","Because the gas gravity rises with depth, which stiffens the gas.","Because the gas bulk modulus rises from 17.7074 to 111.3929 MPa, so the gap to a brine near 2697.8113 MPa narrows."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same gas is a very different target shallow and deep, because pressure stiffens it steeply. Gas density also rises with depth rather than falling, from 69.5252 to 237.8996 kg/m3, which rules out the first option.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A brine property set measured on a bench at 20 degC gives a bulk modulus of 2.511437 GPa and is used for a reservoir at 60 degC. The capstone grades the brine bulk modulus as 2.6978112899395996 GPa. What is the verdict?', '["It fails, and by far more than the 0.005 GPa window the field is graded to.","It passes, because the two values agree to two significant figures.","It passes, provided the measurement temperature is recorded beside it.","The tolerance does not apply, because a measured value outranks a computed one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gap between the bench value and the in situ value is orders of magnitude larger than the tolerance on that field, which is the tightest of the six. Recording the wrong conditions honestly does not make the number usable at 60 degC.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'A learner enters the brine salinity as 10 wt% instead of 3.5 wt% and leaves everything else correct. Which graded capstone fields are damaged?', '["Only the frame bulk modulus, since salinity feeds the mineral mix.","The brine density and the brine bulk modulus, and through them the Wood mixed fluid as well.","Only the gas bulk modulus, since gas and brine share the pore space.","None of them, since salinity affects the velocity but not the density or the modulus."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 10 wt% the brine reads 1063.6947 kg/m3 and 3.034253 GPa in place of 1017.8249875 kg/m3 and 2.6978112899395996 GPa, and Wood''s equation takes both of those as inputs. The frame is a mineral mix and the gas is computed from gravity, so neither sees the salinity.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Which of the six graded capstone values is untouched by every condition dial covered in the conditions module?', '["The brine density, since density is set by salinity rather than by temperature.","The Wood mixed fluid, since a harmonic average cancels the condition dependence.","The frame bulk modulus of 30.87940062475596 GPa, since it is a mix of mineral constants and not a fluid.","The gas bulk modulus, since gas is treated as ideal at this tier."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Temperature, pressure, salinity and GOR are fluid inputs, and the frame is built from fixed mineral moduli and the 70/30 fractions alone. Brine density does move with temperature, from 1032.1697 to 995.3571 kg/m3 across the sweep, and gas moves steeply with pressure.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'A colleague reports a gas bulk modulus as a small decimal with several leading zeros after the point. What is the most likely explanation?', '["The gas is nearly incompressible at reservoir pressure.","They have read the mixed fluid line rather than the gas line.","They have quoted a compliance rather than a modulus.","They have quoted it in GPa, where the capstone grades it in MPa as 55.71865290286663."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gas is quoted in MPa throughout this course precisely because the GPa form is a string of leading zeros that is easy to mistype. A value in the wrong band by a factor of a thousand is a unit error rather than a physics result.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Rank the three Ekene fluids from softest to stiffest at 60 degC and 25 MPa.', '["Gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa, live oil at 1.1427945726905131 GPa, brine at 2.6978112899395996 GPa.","Live oil at 1.1427945726905131 GPa, gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa, brine at 2.6978112899395996 GPa.","Brine at 2.6978112899395996 GPa, gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa, live oil at 1.1427945726905131 GPa.","Gas at 55.71865290286663 MPa, brine at 2.6978112899395996 GPa, live oil at 1.1427945726905131 GPa."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gas is softest by a wide margin, with brine 48.42 times stiffer than it at these conditions, and the oil sits between the two. The second option is the unit trap, reading 55.71865290286663 MPa as though it outranked 1.1427945726905131 GPa.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'A producing reservoir is depleted, so its pore pressure falls while the overburden above it does not change. What happens to the fluids and to the rock frame?', '["Both soften, because falling pressure relaxes the whole system.","The fluids soften along the pressure rows while the effective stress rises and the frame stiffens, so the two push the seismic response in opposite directions.","Both stiffen, because the effective stress rises on the fluids as well as on the frame.","Neither changes, because depletion moves the saturations rather than the pressures."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pore pressure is the fluid input and effective stress is the overburden minus it, so depletion moves the two in opposite directions. That opposition is one reason time lapse work is harder than it looks.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'The mineral fractions are typed as 60 percent quartz and 40 percent clay instead of 70 and 30. Which quality control check catches that most directly?', '["The unit band check, since the frame moduli would leave the tens of GPa band.","The harmonic mixing check, since the mixed fluid would move away from the gas end.","The frame density check, since the density must come back as exactly 2629 kg/m3.","The conditions check, since the mineral split is part of the stated conditions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Frame density mixes exactly, so it is the one check with no tolerance to hide behind and it fails loudly on a wrong split. The mixed fluid does not see the mineral fractions at all, and both frame moduli would still land in the tens of GPa.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'As GOR rises from 0 to 150 L/L the live oil density falls from 820.9856 to 693.8631 kg/m3, yet the velocity also falls, from 1340.5357 to 1015.7240 m/s. Why does a lighter oil end up slower?', '["Because the temperature rises along with the gas oil ratio.","Because velocity depends on density alone, so a lighter oil is always slower.","Because the salinity of the associated brine rises with the gas oil ratio.","Because the bulk modulus falls harder, from 1.475341 to 0.715855 GPa, so the softening beats the lightening in the square root of K over rho."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Velocity carries both properties, and here the two effects oppose each other with the stiffness winning. The same reasoning explains why the brine velocity peaks one row later than the brine stiffness in the temperature sweep.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'What exactly does the Beginner tier hand on to the tier above it?', '["Three fluids at in situ conditions, a mineral frame and a mixed pore fluid, which are the ingredients Gassmann''s equation needs.","A dry frame bulk modulus recovered from the log point.","An AVO intercept and gradient for the brine and gas cases.","A wedge tuning thickness for the reservoir interval."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Beginner tier builds ingredients rather than a rock or a seismic response. Recovering the dry frame belongs to the Professional tier, and the AVO and tuning work belongs to the Expert tier.'),
  ('rockphysics', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which statement best expresses the rule that the conditions module ends on?', '["A fluid property is acceptable without its conditions provided it is quoted to enough digits.","A fluid property quoted without its temperature, pressure and composition is not a result, since the same brine spans 2.511437 to 2.697811 GPa over 20 to 100 degC and 2.534420 to 3.611999 GPa over 0 to 20 wt% salt.","A fluid property measured in a laboratory can be carried to reservoir conditions unchanged.","Conditions matter for gas because it is compressible, and not for brine or oil."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every condition dial moves the answer by more than any capstone tolerance, so the rule follows without any argument about significant figures. Brine and oil are as condition dependent as gas, which rules out the last option.');
